.TH BG-VNCPRIVACY 1 "November 2026" "bg-vncPrivacy" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bg-vncPrivacy \- keep the local monitor dark while working over VNC
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bg-vncPrivacy
.RI [ command ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bg-vncPrivacy
hides the desktop from anyone at the physical monitor while you work on it
over VNC.
.I Privacy on
switches the monitor, over DDC/CI, to an input with nothing connected, so it
shows nothing whatever the desktop does.
.I Privacy off
switches it back to the input this computer is connected to.
.PP
x11vnc calls the program when VNC clients connect and disconnect. If the local
monitor is off when the first client connects, privacy turns on, so working
remotely does not light it up. When the last client disconnects with privacy
on, the screen is locked, the display powered down and the monitor switched
back, so it stays off. With privacy off, connecting and disconnecting leave
the monitor alone.
.PP
While a VNC session is active, a
.B VNC Privacy
item (monitor icon, slashed while privacy is on) appears in the top bar. A
single click opens a menu with the current state and a
.I Turn local monitor on/off
item. A double- or middle-click toggles directly.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B tray
Run the top-bar item (the default). Started at desktop login by
.IR /etc/xdg/autostart/bg-vncPrivacy.desktop .
Only one runs per session.
.TP
.B vncSessionStart
x11vnc
.B \-afteraccept
hook. Counts a client in. For the first client, turns privacy on if the
desktop reports the display blanked or powered down.
.TP
.B vncSessionEnd
x11vnc
.B \-gone
hook. Counts a client out. After the last client, if privacy is on, locks the
screen, powers the display down and switches the monitor back.
.TP
.B on
Turn privacy on (only while a VNC session is active).
.TP
.B off
Turn privacy off. Always switches the monitor back, so it also rescues a
monitor left on the unused input.
.TP
.B toggle
Switch between on and off.
.TP
.B status
Print the session count, the privacy state, what the desktop reports about
the display, and the last error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/bg-vncPrivacy.conf
Monitor serial number
.RB ( [monitor]\ serial ),
the input codes for the desk and private inputs
.RB ( desk_input ,
.BR private_input ;
DDC/CI VCP feature 0x60) and the VNC port
.RB ( [vnc]\ port ).
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bg-vncPrivacy/
State: session count, privacy state, last error.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/x11vnc-console
x11vnc launcher; the installer adds the hooks to it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find the values for the configuration file:
.PP
.nf
.RS
ddcutil detect
ddcutil \-\-sn SERIAL capabilities
ddcutil \-\-sn SERIAL getvcp 60
.RE
.fi
.PP
Rescue a monitor stuck on the private input, over ssh:
.PP
.RS
.B ssh host bg-vncPrivacy off
.RE
.SH NOTES
Changing the display's power state from software does not keep the monitor
dark, because VNC input counts as user activity and GNOME wakes the display.
Switching the monitor's input is not affected by that.
.PP
DDC/CI access comes from the
.B ddcutil
package's udev rule, which gives the user logged in at the console access to
.IR /dev/i2c\-* .
.SH BUGS
At the login screen there is no top-bar item and no GNOME session to ask, so
privacy never turns on automatically there.
.PP
GNOME's AppIndicator extension shows no hover tooltips, so the state is shown
in the menu.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR x11vnc (1),
.BR ddcutil (1)
